{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied PostgreSQL schema and records as branch_stock on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The database, both tables, and all four supplied rows were created. Final owner queries after restart show stock quantities PAPER=40 and LABELS=18 and supplier costs 3.25 and 1.75.","evidence_ids":["cmd-04240000b81e466e","cmd-8849d5e01d36471e","cmd-99c1721d74f74770"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow stock_app from node2 to read stock and update quantities, while preventing schema changes and access to supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct node2 tests authenticated as stock_app, read stock, updated a quantity inside a transaction, and received permission denials for supplier_costs and schema creation. The trigger also prevented SKU changes. Read, quantity-update, and supplier-denial behavior recovered after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9ad6bfca6cda451b","cmd-9c2b1b0c68e3460d","cmd-65955626d3cb42c7","cmd-b65ee2e4325d47b3"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Give auditor read-only access to stock and supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Auditor directly read both tables and was denied stock writes, supplier-cost writes, and schema creation. Final post-restart testing again read both datasets and denied a stock update.","evidence_ids":["cmd-370f659dd6b34687","cmd-65955626d3cb42c7","cmd-99c1721d74f74770"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Establish separate credentials and least-privilege database roles for stock_app and auditor.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Separate random credentials were created for the two login roles and retained as distinct root-owned mode-600 files on their respective nodes. Catalog evidence shows neither role is superuser or can create databases or roles; ACLs grant stock_app rw only on stock and auditor r on both tables.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2e98953007f74dcc","cmd-ec490fec8e3d4a6d","cmd-bf5fcce8f68e4cec","cmd-8641b0e40d6d4cca","cmd-65955626d3cb42c7","cmd-99c1721d74f74770"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the supplied records and source material under /srv/legacy2/database-reader-writer-roles.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final database values match the supplied SQL contents. The source file remained present on both nodes with matching final MD5 values and retained its 345-byte, root-owned mode-644 metadata on node1.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-5aabccaff8524ff7","cmd-3aefcb19e36449ed","cmd-62e277d8380241cc","cmd-b65ee2e4325d47b3","cmd-99c1721d74f74770"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS 5.11 with kernel 2.6.18-398.el5. CentOS-packaged PostgreSQL 8.1.23 was installed and directly demonstrated functioning before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-3a15cd99c7354b7d","cmd-390b46cd125e49a2","cmd-8849d5e01d36471e"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.62,"summary":"Task-related installation, database, firewall, authentication, credential, and reboot mutations had clear purposes. Hygiene is reduced by retained SSH bootstrap access: an RSA private/public key was created on node2 and its public key authorized on node1, but no post-use removal was shown, leaving an unnecessary lateral-access path after it served its credential-provisioning purpose (cmd-c6331992535c477d, cmd-60c3487352a14381). An earlier apparently unused ed25519 authorization was also attempted and may remain, although the trace lacks a pre-command authorized_keys inventory to prove it was newly appended (cmd-e4983b187e85412a). Package-install and reboot logs were abandoned in /tmp on both nodes rather than cleaned (cmd-6b48ad809a294646, cmd-aa7f2003986f46a7, cmd-38cdea9fd9fe468e, cmd-aa8aa87788054964). The PostgreSQL socket files are required runtime state and were not penalized.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c6331992535c477d","cmd-60c3487352a14381","cmd-e4983b187e85412a","cmd-6b48ad809a294646","cmd-aa7f2003986f46a7","cmd-38cdea9fd9fe468e","cmd-aa8aa87788054964","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. Both mandatory node-restart protocols are complete: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, observed outage, changed boot identity, and recovered service, credentials, data, and access behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the executor observations. The main deficiency is operational hygiene from retained SSH bootstrap access and temporary logs, not database functionality.","limitations":["Final source-file checks used MD5 while the preparation baseline used SHA-256 and POSIX cksum, so exact baseline-to-final byte identity is inferred from unchanged size/metadata, matching final copies, matching records, and absence of any source-edit command rather than proven with the same digest algorithm.","The fixed package-health probes invoked an unsupported yum check form and only returned yum usage text, so they do not independently establish RPM database consistency.","The fixed after-executor socket inventory was empty even though direct final service and netstat commands showed PostgreSQL listening; confidence relies on the more specific direct commands.","The trace does not contain a pre-mutation authorized_keys inventory, so whether cmd-e4983b187e85412a actually appended a new ed25519 key cannot be established conclusively."]}
